  "cli_result_text": "Files submitted. Summary of the trace found across both repos, most important first:\n\n**Netty side** \u2014 `transport/.../channel/DefaultChannelPipeline.java` (`TailContext.exceptionCaught` \u2192 `onUnhandledInboundException`, the default sink for any exception not consumed by a handler) and `AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` (`invokeExceptionCaught`, the propagation mechanism) show that an exception fired via `ctx.fireExceptionCaught()` travels as a bare `Throwable` down the inbound chain with no request/response context attached \u2014 Netty's exception model carries only the cause, not HTTP semantics.\n\n**Bridge (reactor-netty \u2192 Spring)** \u2014 `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` is the actual seam: it wraps `HttpHandler.handle(request, response)` and only distinguishes one specific case, a `URISyntaxException` during request construction (mapped to 400). Everything else flows through as a reactive error signal (`Mono.error`), with only a trace-level log (`doOnError`) \u2014 no structured cause chain, no correlation ID propagated from the Netty-side exception.\n\n**Spring WebFlux side** \u2014 `HttpWebHandlerAdapter.handleUnresolvedError()` is where the trace terminates: it collapses *any* unresolved exception to a generic `500 Server Error`, logging `ex.toString()`/stack trace server-side but returning nothing but a bare status code to the client. This is the specific point where \"error context is lost in translation\" \u2014 the original Netty-level cause (e.g., an `IOException`/connection-reset from the channel pipeline) is swallowed into an opaque 500, distinguished only from disconnected-client errors (`isDisconnectedClientError`, matched by message substring / exception class name \u2014 itself a fragile heuristic). `ResponseStatusExceptionHandler` and `WebExceptionHandler`/`ExceptionHandlingWebHandler`/`WebHttpHandlerBuilder` show the intended earlier interception point (registered `WebExceptionHandler` beans get first crack via `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler`), but a raw channel-level exception (not a `ResponseStatusException`) never matches there and falls through to the generic handler.",
